    content:
      "Roman-style carbonara gets its silky sauce from nothing more than eggs, Pecorino and rendered guanciale fat emulsified with starchy pasta water, no cream required.\n\n### Ingredients:\n\n* 200 g spaghetti\n* 100 g guanciale (or thick-cut pancetta), cut into 1 cm × 0.5 cm strips\n* 1 large egg plus 2 large egg yolks, at room temperature\n* 50 g Pecorino Romano, finely grated, plus extra to serve\n* 1 tsp (5 ml) freshly cracked black pepper, plus extra to serve\n* About 20 g coarse salt for the pasta water\n\n### Instructions:\n\n1. Bring 2 l of water to the boil in a large pot and add the salt.\n2. Meanwhile, place the guanciale in a cold 26–28 cm frying pan, set over medium-low heat and render for 8–10 minutes, stirring occasionally, until the fat is translucent and the edges are golden and crisp.\n3. Add the cracked pepper to the pan for the last 30 seconds to toast it, then turn off the heat.\n4. In a bowl, whisk the egg, yolks and Pecorino into a thick paste; it should look like a loose porridge.\n5. Cook the spaghetti for 1 minute less than the packet time (usually 8–9 minutes) until just al dente. Reserve 200 ml of the pasta water before draining.\n6. Whisk 2–3 tbsp (30–45 ml) of the hot pasta water into the egg mixture to temper it.\n7. Transfer the drained spaghetti into the pan with the guanciale (heat still off), add 2 tbsp (30 ml) pasta water and toss well to coat in the fat. Let it cool for about 1 minute so the pan is warm, not sizzling.\n8. Pour in the egg mixture and toss vigorously with tongs for 1–2 minutes, adding splashes of pasta water as needed, until the sauce turns glossy and creamy and clings to the strands without scrambling (the sauce should reach about 65–70°C).\n9. Serve immediately in warmed bowls topped with extra Pecorino and black pepper.\n\n### Tips:\n\n* If the eggs start to look grainy, the pan was too hot; add a splash of pasta water and toss off the heat to rescue it.\n* Pancetta works if you cannot find guanciale, but avoid smoked bacon, which overpowers the dish.\n* Carbonara does not hold or reheat well; serve it the moment it is sauced.",
