    content:
      "French Onion Soup turns a big pile of slowly caramelised onions into a deep, sweet beef broth, crowned with toasted baguette and bubbling Gruyère.\n\n### Ingredients:\n\n* 900 g yellow onions (about 5 medium), halved and thinly sliced\n* 40 g unsalted butter\n* 1 tbsp (15 ml) olive oil\n* 1 tsp (5 ml) salt, plus more to taste\n* 1/2 tsp (2.5 ml) sugar\n* 2 garlic cloves, finely chopped\n* 1 tbsp (8 g) plain flour\n* 100 ml dry white wine\n* 1 l good-quality beef stock\n* 3 sprigs fresh thyme\n* 1 bay leaf\n* 1 tsp (5 ml) Worcestershire sauce or a splash of brandy (optional)\n* Freshly ground black pepper\n* 6 slices baguette, about 1.5 cm thick\n* 120 g Gruyère (or Comté), coarsely grated\n\n### Instructions:\n\n1. Melt the butter with the oil in a heavy 24–26 cm casserole over medium heat. Add the onions and salt and stir to coat.\n2. Cook for 10 minutes, stirring occasionally, until the onions are soft and have released their liquid.\n3. Add the sugar and continue cooking over medium to medium-low heat for 25–30 minutes, stirring every few minutes and scraping up the brown bits, until the onions are a deep, even mahogany colour and jammy. Add a splash of water if the base starts to scorch.\n4. Stir in the garlic and flour and cook for 1 minute.\n5. Pour in the wine and scrape the bottom of the pan to deglaze, then simmer for 1–2 minutes until mostly evaporated.\n6. Add the stock, thyme, bay leaf and Worcestershire. Simmer gently, uncovered, for 10–15 minutes. Remove the herbs and season with salt and pepper.\n7. Meanwhile, heat the oven grill to high (about 230°C). Toast the baguette slices on a tray for 1–2 minutes per side until dry and lightly golden.\n8. Ladle the soup into 3 ovenproof bowls set on a baking tray. Float 2 toasts on each and cover generously with the Gruyère.\n9. Grill for 3–4 minutes until the cheese is melted, bubbling and browned in spots. Serve carefully, as the bowls will be very hot.\n\n### Tips:\n\n* Do not rush the caramelisation with high heat; the onions will burn on the outside before they sweeten.\n* The soup base (without bread and cheese) keeps for 4 days in the fridge and freezes for 3 months, and tastes even better the next day.\n* For a vegetarian version, use a rich mushroom stock and add 1 tsp (5 ml) soy sauce for depth.",
    servings: 3,
    slug: "french-onion-soup",
    time: 60,
